In 1966 National, afraid the seat would go to Labour, poured ''thousands of dollars'' into the local campaign. A hundred women from all over Waikato canvassed every house in Tokoroa and Putāruru over two days, using the ''street lists and blue dot'' system. The seat was held by 258 votes.<ref>{{cite book |last= Gustafson |author-link= Barry Gustafson |first= Barry |title= The First 50 Years: A History of the New Zealand National Party |year= 1986 |publisher= Reed Methuen |location= Auckland |isbn= 0-474-00177-6 |pages= 91,280 }}</ref>

